WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:13.257
Idea. Think of a café menu. Some items sell every single day. Some are new and still finding fans. Some hardly ever sell. All the products a business sells are called its product portfolio.

00:00:13.850 --> 00:00:30.252
The Boston matrix sorts these products using two measures. Market growth: is the market getting bigger? And market share: how much of that market does the product have? Growth runs up the side, and share runs along the bottom, with high share on the left.

00:00:31.400 --> 00:00:48.781
Method. Step one: place the product by asking two questions. Is market growth high or low? Is market share high or low? Step two: name the category and its decision. High growth and high share makes a star. Stars are usually worth investing in.

00:00:49.350 --> 00:00:56.132
Low growth but high share makes a cash cow. It brings in steady profit, which can fund other products.

00:00:56.700 --> 00:01:07.941
High growth but low share makes a question mark, sometimes called a problem child. Its future is uncertain. It may need investment to succeed, or it may be dropped.

00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:13.393
Low growth and low share makes a dog. Dogs are often dropped or replaced.

00:01:13.950 --> 00:01:25.809
Step three: link the decision to the whole portfolio. Profit from cash cows funds stars, question marks and new products, so the range stays balanced across several categories.

00:01:26.950 --> 00:01:41.381
Worked example. Here is a real question from Paper one in June 2022. Explain one benefit to a business of using the Boston matrix. It is worth three marks: one benefit, then two linked steps.

00:01:41.950 --> 00:01:48.361
Mark one is the benefit. The Boston matrix helps a business keep a balanced product portfolio.

00:01:48.950 --> 00:01:59.145
Mark two shows what the matrix reveals. Managers can see if they rely too heavily on a few cash cows, with too few stars or question marks coming through.

00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:09.695
Mark three gives the action and result. The business uses cash cow profit to launch new products before older ones fall away, so its income stays steady.

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:22.648
Common mistake. Mistake one: swapping the stars and cash cows. Both have high market share. The difference is growth: stars are in growing markets, cash cows are not.

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:29.523
Mistake two: thinking the matrix shows profit directly. It measures market share and market growth.

00:02:30.650 --> 00:02:44.354
Your turn. Now you try. A games company has a football game with a large share of a market that has stopped growing. Where does it go on the Boston matrix, and what should the company do with it? Pause and decide.

00:02:45.500 --> 00:02:56.563
Answer. Low growth and high share make it a cash cow. The company should keep selling it and use its steady profit to develop new games, perhaps for faster-growing markets.

00:02:57.150 --> 00:03:05.636
Remember the method: ask about growth, ask about share, then name the category and its decision. Now try the practice questions.
